  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A particle moves with constant acceleration. The velocity of the particle equals $(-4i+9j)\\,\\text{ft/s}$ both when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ and $t=7\\,\\text{s}.$ What is the acceleration of the particle?\n\n$a=($\n{{field-1}}$\\,i$\n$+$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-4i+9j,\\,v=-4i+9j,\\,t=7\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ -4i+9j & =(-4i+9j)+a(7) \\\\ 0 & =7a \\\\ a & =(0\\,i+0\\,j)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
